WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.845
Earlier in this lesson, each check became a value, like a card from a recipe drawer.

2
00:00:07.845 --> 00:00:11.520
You can hold it, keep it in a list, and lend it to a friend.

3
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.685
Now pin a note to one card: my oven runs hot, take five minutes off.

4
00:00:15.685 --> 00:00:20.647
The note was written where the card was made, and it goes wherever the card goes.

5
00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:23.642
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:23.642 --> 00:00:27.021
A lambda uses a variable from the function that made it.

7
00:00:27.021 --> 00:00:29.374
But that function has already returned.

8
00:00:29.374 --> 00:00:31.546
So where does the variable live now?

9
00:00:31.546 --> 00:00:35.769
And if two people hold the card, does each of them get their own note?

10
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:42.597
Let's shrink it to the smallest closure there is.

11
00:00:42.597 --> 00:00:46.303
makeCounter has a local variable, count, starting at zero.

12
00:00:46.303 --> 00:00:50.200
It returns a lambda that adds one to count and hands it back.

13
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.394
Call that lambda three times, and you get 1, 2, 3.

14
00:00:53.394 --> 00:00:56.972
So count is still alive, after makeCounter has returned.

15
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.523
Java won't even compile the same idea.

16
00:01:00.523 --> 00:01:07.151
It says: local variables referenced from a lambda expression must be final or effectively final.

17
00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.646
The reason is mechanical.

18
00:01:09.646 --> 00:01:15.902
A local variable lives in the method's stack frame, the memory one method call uses for its locals.

19
00:01:15.902 --> 00:01:18.746
When makeCounter returns, that frame is gone.

20
00:01:18.746 --> 00:01:22.600
But the lambda lives on, and it would be pointing at nothing.

21
00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:28.179
So Java makes you build the box yourself: a one element array, or an AtomicInteger.

22
00:01:28.179 --> 00:01:35.071
Either one is an object on the heap, the memory where objects live for as long as something points at them.

23
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.073
Kotlin builds that box for you, silently.

24
00:01:38.073 --> 00:01:44.500
The compiler moves count into a small heap object, a Ref$IntRef, with one field called element.

25
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.949
makeCounter and the lambda both use that one field.

26
00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:51.490
And the field is a plain int: no volatile, no lock.

27
00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.119
Java refuses the capture.

28
00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:56.117
The Kotlin compiler shrugs and builds the box.

29
00:01:56.117 --> 00:01:59.962
So the point really is: the note isn't written on the card.

30
00:01:59.962 --> 00:02:03.155
It sits in a box, and the card points at the box.

31
00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:04.878
Let's map it.

32
00:02:04.878 --> 00:02:06.751
The card is the lambda's code.

33
00:02:06.751 --> 00:02:08.873
The note is the captured variable.

34
00:02:08.873 --> 00:02:11.120
Pinning the note on is capturing it.

35
00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.553
And the box on the heap is where the note really lives.

36
00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:19.409
Here the card picture breaks, and it breaks in a way that matters.

37
00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:23.410
Lend a paper card, and your friend gets their own copy of the note.

38
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.516
Hand out a closure, and everyone holds the same box.

39
00:02:27.433 --> 00:02:29.170
On one thread, that's fine.

40
00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:34.765
A thread is one path of work through a program, and several can run at the same moment.

41
00:02:34.765 --> 00:02:37.851
So let's share one closure between four threads.

42
00:02:37.851 --> 00:02:41.388
Each one does total += 1, a quarter of a million times.

43
00:02:41.388 --> 00:02:43.253
Four quarters make a million.

44
00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:45.927
What does total hold when all four are done?

45
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:50.953
Here's the catch.

46
00:02:50.953 --> 00:02:56.206
total += 1 is three steps: read the field, add one, write it back.

47
00:02:56.206 --> 00:02:59.708
Two threads read the same old value, say 41.

48
00:02:59.708 --> 00:03:00.743
Both add one.

49
00:03:00.743 --> 00:03:01.857
Both write 42.

50
00:03:01.857 --> 00:03:04.723
Two additions, and only one counted.

51
00:03:05.633 --> 00:03:12.427
The lesson's demo ran it ten times on a busy four core machine.

52
00:03:12.427 --> 00:03:14.368
Run 1 got 250,956.

53
00:03:14.368 --> 00:03:16.093
It lost 749,044.

54
00:03:16.093 --> 00:03:19.975
Runs 2 to 10 were all exactly right.

55
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:20.506
Well.

56
00:03:20.506 --> 00:03:25.152
Three quarters of the count are gone, and nine runs out of ten look perfect.

57
00:03:25.152 --> 00:03:28.821
That's the worst kind of race: it passes most of your tests.

58
00:03:28.821 --> 00:03:34.018
Kotlin compiled all of it without a word, because nothing in the source looks shared.

59
00:03:34.018 --> 00:03:35.913
The sharing comes from the box.

60
00:03:36.833 --> 00:03:37.695
Now the name.

61
00:03:37.695 --> 00:03:41.404
Code plus the variables it captured is called a closure.

62
00:03:41.404 --> 00:03:44.186
And a captured var becomes one shared box.

63
00:03:44.186 --> 00:03:47.697
Everyone holding the closure writes on the same note.

64
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:49.887
There are two fixes.

65
00:03:49.887 --> 00:03:53.039
One: share a thread safe object instead of a var.

66
00:03:53.039 --> 00:03:59.151
Capture a val that holds an AtomicLong, and call incrementAndGet, one step that can't be split.

67
00:03:59.151 --> 00:04:01.468
Two, and better: don't share at all.

68
00:04:01.468 --> 00:04:05.842
Let each task count its own work, and add up the results at the end.

69
00:04:05.842 --> 00:04:08.416
Both print exactly a million, every run.

70
00:04:09.333 --> 00:04:10.588
So here's the rule.

71
00:04:10.588 --> 00:04:15.012
A captured var is one box that everyone holding the closure shares.

72
00:04:15.012 --> 00:04:17.191
Never change it from two threads.

73
00:04:17.191 --> 00:04:26.171
In your own code, look for a var declared just before a lambda that runs somewhere else: inside submit, inside thread, or in a callback.

74
00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.955
So, where does the variable live once the function returns?

75
00:04:30.955 --> 00:04:32.327
In a box on the heap.

76
00:04:32.327 --> 00:04:35.725
And two people holding the card don't get two notes.

77
00:04:35.725 --> 00:04:36.705
They share one.

78
00:04:36.933 --> 00:04:37.807
Quick check.

79
00:04:37.807 --> 00:04:41.591
a and b come from two separate calls to makeCounter.

80
00:04:41.591 --> 00:04:43.556
You call a, then a, then b.

81
00:04:43.556 --> 00:04:44.939
What does b return?

82
00:04:47.933 --> 00:04:48.076
1.

83
00:04:48.076 --> 00:04:51.143
Each call to makeCounter makes its own box.

84
00:04:51.143 --> 00:04:54.567
Sharing happens only when one closure is shared.
